[{"id":"VRCE-2-95-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":1,"english":"Hearing Rāma's words, Bharata answered, Deprived of the kingdom in consequence of my posteriority in point of birth, what does regard for morality avail me?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-102-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d1c97ffa7eab2e8a93a023c18149e628c2a37ccb893a57c362fc58b4e0da6573","compact":"e393f0ca86235c4ec8b52772779a76f98cdfac9be8df967404a0131261290486","nasal":"e393f0ca86235c4ec8b52772779a76f98cdfac9be8df967404a0131261290486"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":2,"english":"O best of men, even this morality has ever been established with reference to us, viz., that the eldest son of the king existing, a younger one cannot be the king.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.797,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-102-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b01301e71e8ad6f1e221dda91a1a4fac55b1cf5ea89fdaf0b8e542dad2b73c0a","compact":"145ad95fb5d17c62ecb62e5c4e8150d120590e2023d9e4a7eb4292077dbd23ba","nasal":"9c68622b247d40b90dfc0df7dd3e8171d995df76798c1032e83f6ebec40f3bf3"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":3,"english":"Do you, therefore, O Rāghava go along with me to the prosperous Ayodhya; and get yourself ir stalled there for the behoof of our race.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-102-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7f54cef3e2b54e10a0519f2f3e53a3587b0dce222ec8b00d6413de0dfb626800","compact":"c7dd17dd81bb07ec45a5c015a7c8e4bead4034a7b65d75cf5939e499b57ec61a","nasal":"c7dd17dd81bb07ec45a5c015a7c8e4bead4034a7b65d75cf5939e499b57ec61a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":4,"english":"Although a king observing interest and virtue, and who towers above average humanity, has been called a mortal, yet to me he is a very deity.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.869,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-102-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ff1f3cbfc5f3b7a4cb1f1b0fbf10178f1f0867dbde0b8d866322c73028aca3fb","compact":"542e5cc20fc29206c65f83c4fafe14bd955a2d93b45c3af1bda9ce625a554471","nasal":"542e5cc20fc29206c65f83c4fafe14bd955a2d93b45c3af1bda9ce625a554471"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":5,"english":"While I was in Kekaya and you was in the forest that intelligent monarch honoured of the good, given to celebrating sacrifices, ascended heaven.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.727,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-102-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"dcc45b18b1d001ca1402bf1b37bb3a7172301a18c906df8e5b70c813abd42fa6","compact":"a0431b8a306ffc277339d80b493c2c76564509a34717867639570d01698bc3ea","nasal":"a0431b8a306ffc277339d80b493c2c76564509a34717867639570d01698bc3ea"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":6,"english":"O foremost of men, do you arise, and offer water to the spirit of our sire. Śatrughna and I have ere this offered water to (the departed).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-102-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"80d04f0cb83fafccfdf1c9cf182d37f5ea236d6c0b41b3d4950073cbd9c46fdc","compact":"3a45150d3f9c92a550a8f500ed692c638eb185abe2718c425bf04086bc9a5fcc","nasal":"3a45150d3f9c92a550a8f500ed692c638eb185abe2718c425bf04086bc9a5fcc"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":7,"english":"O Rāghava, anything offered to the (manes of the) ancestors by a beloved descendant, conduces to their eternal behoof; and you the favourite of our father.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.766,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-102-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c939ee2e3a12acc96862d4dacd0dee1ad44c4f677192d4a65464dca49a68b031","compact":"55b19becb621740495984fc7eb5b6eb82d99840f7314abd11f0e8fed89cdeaed","nasal":"55b19becb621740495984fc7eb5b6eb82d99840f7314abd11f0e8fed89cdeaed"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":8,"english":"Hearing those piteous words uttered by Bharata in connection with the demise of their father, Rāghava was deprived of his senses.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b3c672c2e3b6fe6f1e89eaa4cfd5076393f93500a4c9c41363a765e7977423f8","compact":"8ffd5eac55d12d13320efb2ba793a813aa48bc3886b29d485c8e286f7de5f8f6","nasal":"8ffd5eac55d12d13320efb2ba793a813aa48bc3886b29d485c8e286f7de5f8f6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":9,"english":"On that thunder-bolt of a speech being uttered by Bharata, like to a (real) thunder-bolt hurled in battle by the enemy of the Dānavas (Indra), that subduer of foes, Rāma, stretching his arms, fell down to the earth, like a blossoming tree that has been hewn by an axe.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.565,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1f26fea274ed062f7fe88c363187c12872ab9b3478a55ae2ad5a6c7640b8681b","compact":"982848e0b9d54fade736e3c23fc543788f22dc3ccfa86e012e7ab7a03be3e79a","nasal":"982848e0b9d54fade736e3c23fc543788f22dc3ccfa86e012e7ab7a03be3e79a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":10,"english":"Seeing that lord of the world and mighty bowman, Rāma, fallen, like a sleeping elephant fatigued with turning up earth with its tusks, his brothers exercised with sorrow, broke out into lamentations, and along with Videha's daughter began to sprinkle water (on his face).","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a487ac0383a67e6fe1febc8d29b7d4fa4087a6f525bd657d1b9b27a634592592","compact":"dc1a0b48039833068623f1c5aec6d5053311d0db016d5a5e840c41c9d58ba7e9","nasal":"c565e76a0ab2583d665c413d2d2de342f43feb4919e0d27f528c2b45a98b2ebc"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":11,"english":"Seeing that lord of the world and mighty bowman, Rāma, fallen, like a sleeping elephant fatigued with turning up earth with its tusks, his brothers exercised with sorrow, broke out into lamentations, and along with Videha's daughter began to sprinkle water (on his face).","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.869,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"dc82ea8607f5ce9a8d3d1508182a4a4df24c04a2e90ab4829c35206a147ab529","compact":"6a5b9aba80a4c86977dc43c4edfafc65ad8969a65b70d150f0979ed9c1bf89c7","nasal":"0b703fb85fcd87467f3705bf6994e8691621124a527ae56d7a3596522fa1ab68"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-12","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":12,"english":"Then regaining his consciousness, Kākutstha shedding tears from his eyes, distressfully addressed himself to speech.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.743,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095012","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0f0ec625693dd84388da867eb2c5a94fbe47149e59a7e8b87ed9b2a6b36049fa","compact":"c7ebaeea8a53195de803516474b6582ff1cf8bd84c3326bb63a8d731b6a3a147","nasal":"c7ebaeea8a53195de803516474b6582ff1cf8bd84c3326bb63a8d731b6a3a147"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-13","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":13,"english":"What shall I do with Ayodhyā, my sire having gone the way ordained by the gods? And who will govern her, now that she has been deprived of that foremost of monarchs? Of fruitless birth that I am, what can I do for that magnanimous one? And of him that renounced his life from grief on my account I have not even performed the last rites.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.596,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095013","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d970c74c827bff2ed102073f5efa4286247a4115e927299998ca8e62247c8fa2","compact":"624edcfe481edf817b9fdaef5d82e61a96f53276002aac7974ad2099232dfaa6","nasal":"624edcfe481edf817b9fdaef5d82e61a96f53276002aac7974ad2099232dfaa6"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-14","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":14,"english":"Ah! Bharata, you, O sinless one, are blessed, since by you as well as by Satrughna have been performed all the funeral rites of the king.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.847,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095014","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1fbe84a34e7e901e38d40e26eb288b137171ed74dce75748f69d00b389dfee4d","compact":"5df9fbb5abbc0271e95e63a5371a7f7a96fd0eb5ec29ce20cc87be05bb860beb","nasal":"5df9fbb5abbc0271e95e63a5371a7f7a96fd0eb5ec29ce20cc87be05bb860beb"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-15","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":15,"english":"To Ayodhyā, bereft of the monarch, having none to preside over her destinies, and many rulers, will I not return even when the term of my abode in the woods has been passed.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.855,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095015","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bf8033e076497d352b1b9177e1bb522a884ccf012ad5370445ef535d0176ae38","compact":"29ca2d6f2f32abb1c78c58971f231817378051f046cec290b0389fefff1d2392","nasal":"0f83006dc42561726e72c417aa9a3c9957c91e29f591c34be90d4ce3484f2a0f"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-16","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":16,"english":"My father having gone to the other world, who, O subduer of enemies, will again counsel me when, my stay in the forest being over, I shall have returned to Ayodhyā?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095016","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6ab98732192d843c6045e7dbf6d0ea002c6481dba4d4dc465e7818d03dfb6e33","compact":"e54d578edea0de6a804e0aee9d7044f8d4a7820338b4da91e11c5783e0c3a290","nasal":"210d127145e5fa54e4f8d04c61d08b900e839b9c4f5738fc68a6c1dc240e91cb"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-17","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":17,"english":"And from whom shall I hear those words grateful to the ear, which my father gratifying me used to speak to me when I happened to do something well?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095017","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d2ec18665cddb0eb77b62dea72ba60d456f0f180379e3bccdeafd1f5ec67d816","compact":"f7b889a633ab7b6d31652b15126621b4265cbd2ff01a121afc57ac564217635b","nasal":"7da7db5d3ce4720f46d2b61432640c920e6d01520a9212680daab679a6166206"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-18","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":18,"english":"Having thus addressed Bharata, Rāghava burning in grief spoke to his wife, with her countenance resembling the full moon, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.869,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095018","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4d73fb7beb2c8db0b0febb95d2a9518f4f700c6455f9ad235cdbbb3cea8b5777","compact":"e37e69f483234bdc45a693bf9e764e69edcfb2c90d6d90cf08261ef0c25b9467","nasal":"9e1efd44f9e1900d3265bfcb9ba1c88d4c05b1159ade1f05620065e4bd503a6b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-95-19","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":95,"verse":19,"english":"O Sītā, your father-in-law is dead and, O Laks inana, you are fatherless. Bharata has communicated to me that sad intelligence that our father has ascended heaven.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.688,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-103-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2095019","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"17f7a07fecefb22b2